The C program is small — read one int with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m its 4 bytes into a [38;5;10mchar[4][0m, print each as [38;5;10m%02x[0m followed by a newline — but three behaviors needed care to reproduce rather than "fix":[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **scanf failure leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at 0.** On EOF, empty input, or non-numeric input ([38;5;10mabc[0m, a lone [38;5;10m-[0m), the C never assigns, so the program prints [38;5;10m00000000[0m. The Rust [38;5;10mscanf_i32[0m returns [38;5;10mfalse[0m and leaves [38;5;10mx[0m untouched, and I deliberately ignore the return value like the C does.[0m[0m
- **glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m saturates, then truncates.** Because glibc converts via [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, an input like [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999[0m clamps to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m and only then gets stored through an [38;5;10mint*[0m, truncating to [38;5;10m0xffffffff[0m → [38;5;10mffffffff[0m. Accumulating into [38;5;10mi32[0m with wrapping or plain [38;5;10mstr::parse[0m would give a different answer. I accumulate in [38;5;10mi128[0m, saturate at the [38;5;10mi64[0m bounds, then cast to [38;5;10mi32[0m.[0m[0m
- **Whitespace skipping crosses newlines**, including [38;5;10m\v[0m and [38;5;10m\f[0m, so I match C's [38;5;10misspace[0m set rather than Rust's.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Byte order is [38;5;10mto_le_bytes[0m, matching the x86-64 little-endian layout the original was compiled for. The only unsafe-adjacent construct in the C ([38;5;10mmemcpy[0m through a [38;5;10mchar[][0m, which is why [38;5;10m-fno-strict-aliasing[0m is in CMakeLists) becomes a safe [38;5;10mto_le_bytes[0m call.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing worth flagging: the output is target-endianness-dependent by construction. I hardcoded little-endian to match the reference build, so this would diverge from the C if either were run on a big-endian target.[0m[0m
